      "## The hype versus the facts\n The Guardian and other outlets have hosted debates in which tech reporters Aisha Down, Blake Montgomery and Dan Milmo answer readers’ questions about the reality of an AI‑driven doomsday.\nThe reporters note that many of the scenarios—nuclear war, societal collapse or bioweapons—sound “too wooly to really take seriously.” They point out that the challenges of creating new deadly viruses are largely administrative, not technical, and that historical attempts such as Aum Shinrikyo’s botulism and anthrax spray have failed.",
      "## What would an AI apocalypse look like?\n It seems unlikely that AI at this point can significantly change the equation around bioweapons or viruses. One researcher, Jacy Reese Anthis, has highlighted the “staggering gulf between how biological brains evolved and how AI systems are built.”",
      "## Why regulators and journalists are hesitant\nThe Guardian interview highlights a perception that tech CEOs are shocked when the public’s opinion is negative. Critics of the AI industry say that the focus on a potential apocalypse distracts from elements of AI that are already inflicting widespread harm like algorithmic discrimination in housing allotment. They also note that tech reporters feel a responsibility to question the power of “tech bros” and the concentration of wealth, but they admit that “there is, unfortunately, not much that comes to mind” for individuals to influence these issues.",
      "## Practical steps that individuals can take\n Alberichthenibelung asks: What, if anything, can individuals like me do about it? You can vote for politicians that endorse AI regulation.",
      "## Uncertainty and the limits of current AI\nOpenAI might have cracked the Navier‑Stokes problem, but not subjective experience. OpenAI, for example, has solved complex mathematical problems such as Navier‑Stokes but not the subjective aspect of consciousness.",
