      "## Climate Concerns About AI Data Centers",
      "Gore said that the public backlash at planning‑commission meetings has less to do with carbon emissions than with anxiety over automation upending society. In a recent interview with TechCrunch, former Vice‑President Al Gore told reporters that the emissions generated by AI data‑center operations are, in his view, a comparatively small fraction of global greenhouse‑gas output.",
      "Gore explained that the total emissions from all AI data centers combined are only a sliver of what landfills worldwide produce. He also pointed out that the electricity needed for air conditioning in these facilities is far less scrutinized, yet it already outpaces the entire European Union’s annual electricity use. The International Energy Agency projects that air‑conditioning demand could triple by 2050, which would place more strain on power grids than AI data‑center growth over the same period.",
      "Gore noted that some large cloud providers are investing in new methane turbines, which would lock in decades more of fossil‑fuel generation. He prefers power sources that rely on renewables and batteries, stating that renewable energy is increasingly the cheapest option.",
      "## The Bigger AI‑Industry Warning",
      "While emissions may be a concern, Gore said the real alarm should come from within the AI industry itself. Gore said that the growing bipartisan opposition to data centers is driven by underlying concerns about job losses and other threats that experts inside OpenAI and inside Anthropic have been trying to alert the public to.",
      "He referenced remarks made by Dario Amodei of Anthropic, Sam Altman, and Elon Musk that urge a slowdown in the pace of capability gains. Even though some public figures, including former President Trump and Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, have mocked these warnings, Gore takes them seriously.",
      "Gore linked the backlash against AI data‑center construction in the United States to broader anxieties about automation and job loss. He suggested that the growing bipartisan opposition to data‑center development is rooted more in fears of societal disruption than in the environmental impact of the facilities themselves.",
      "## Climate Benefits of AI",
      "Gore said that he doesn't treat AI risk and AI’s climate potential as being in opposition. He cited a study by economist Nicholas Stern of the London School of Economics, which projects that AI‑driven efficiency and waste reduction could lower global emissions by 6 % to 9 % annually over the next decade.",
      "This dual perspective—recognizing both the risks and the benefits—illustrates why a balanced conversation is needed. Gore said that the public backlash is driven by concerns about job losses and other threats, not by carbon emissions.",
      "## Solar Energy as a Game‑Changer",
      "Gore immediately pointed to solar power and said that for him the scope and scale of the solar revolution is the breakout star of the sustainability transition. He called the solar revolution “the breakout star” of the sustainability transition and noted that solar and wind are now at, or near, the cost floor for new electricity generation. This makes them meaningfully cheaper than gas, coal, or nuclear.",
      "Gore also referenced the growing investment flow into renewable‑powered data‑center infrastructure.",
      "## What Readers Should Watch",
      "1. **Industry‑led warnings**: Keep an eye on statements from AI firms about potential societal disruption, as these could shape regulatory and public‑policy responses.",
      "Al Gore’s comments underscore that while data‑center emissions are a legitimate concern, they should be weighed alongside the broader risks AI poses to society. Gore said he expects more companies to move inexorably in that direction simply because renewables are increasingly the cheapest option available."
