      "## Context: A Decade of Public‑Finance Expansion\nOver the past decade, governments in developed economies have expanded the use of public‑financing tools to support the development of clean energy and critical materials technologies. The Center on Global Energy Policy at Columbia University produced a Task Force Report on September 20, 2026 to examine how financing can be better tuned to technology readiness.",
      "## What the Task Force Recommends\n  The authors note that clean‑fuel standards, for example, would gradually reduce the carbon intensity of transportation fuels and can serve as a policy lever once the underlying technology is sufficiently mature.",
      "## Aviation Fuel as a Case Study\nIn September 2022, the Clean Air Task Force released a report on decarbonization challenges in aviation. The report highlights biomass‑based fuel alternatives and the potential of synthetic fuels produced from clean hydrogen and CO₂ captured from the air. But unlike bio-SAF, this option would, in the best case (i.e., where fuel is synthesized from clean hydrogen and CO 2 extracted from the air, with renewable energy used throughout the process), enable sustainable, net-zero aviation. They also point out that modified subsidy tools—where a third party covers per‑unit fuel costs that exceed a set threshold—are gaining traction to lower deployment costs.",
      "## How the Two Stories Connect\n The aviation report cautions that policies should support capture technologies that reduce atmospheric CO₂ without leakage, double‑counting, or unintended harms. The Columbia report echoes this by recommending that financing mechanisms target technologies that are additional to existing carbon removal efforts and subject to safeguards.",
      "## Practical Takeaway for Policymakers\nPolicymakers should map funding programs to TRL stages and incorporate safeguards that prevent double‑counting and leakage.",
      "## What to Watch Next\nThe Task Force’s report was released on September 20, 2026, and the aviation study came out in September 2022."
